  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "👹 Demon Epithet Generator",
      "tagline": "Titles worthy of a demon: the Devourer, Duke of Ashes, Lord of the Ninth Pit."
    },
    "intro": "A <a href=\"demon-name-generator.html\">demon</a>'s name tells you who it is; its title tells you why you should be afraid. This **demon epithet generator** gives you both kinds of title: <a href=\"epithet-generator.html\">epithets</a> like <em>the Devourer</em> or <em>the Unbound</em>, and ranks with a domain, like <em>Duke of Ashes</em>, in the style of the old books of demonology. Add one to your demon's name, or stack a few into a full infernal title.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Titles from real demonology",
        "content": "<p>The habit of giving demons grand titles is centuries old:</p><ul><li><strong>The Ars Goetia</strong>, the first book of the 17th-century <em>Lesser Key of Solomon</em>, lists 72 demons, each with a rank borrowed from nobility: Bael is a king, Marbas a president, Furcas a knight. The <strong>Rank and domain</strong> option builds titles the same way.</li><li><strong>Beelzebub, \"Lord of the Flies\"</strong>, comes from Ba'al Zebub, a god of the Philistine city of Ekron in the Bible.</li><li><strong>Abaddon</strong>, called Apollyon in Greek, is \"the Destroyer\" in the Book of Revelation.</li><li><strong>Legion</strong> takes its name from the line \"My name is Legion: for we are many\" in the Gospel of Mark.</li><li><strong>Lucifer</strong> means \"light-bringer\": the Latin name for the morning star.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The seven princes of sin",
        "content": "<p>In 1589 the German theologian Peter Binsfeld matched a prince of Hell to each of the seven deadly sins, and the list is still the basis of countless games and stories:</p><ul><li><strong>Lucifer</strong>: pride</li><li><strong>Mammon</strong>: greed</li><li><strong>Asmodeus</strong>: lust</li><li><strong>Leviathan</strong>: envy</li><li><strong>Beelzebub</strong>: gluttony</li><li><strong>Satan</strong>: wrath</li><li><strong>Belphegor</strong>: sloth</li></ul><p>The <strong>Sin and vice</strong> category follows the same idea: pick the sin your demon rules, and its epithet does the rest (the Gluttonous, Envy Given Form).</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Demons and devils in D&D",
        "content": "<p>Dungeons &amp; Dragons draws a line worth knowing: <strong>demons</strong> are creatures of chaos from the Abyss, while <strong>devils</strong> are creatures of order from the Nine Hells. Their titles show it:</p><ul><li>Demon lords: Demogorgon, <em>Prince of Demons</em>; Orcus, <em>Prince of Undeath</em>; Graz'zt, <em>the Dark Prince</em>; Juiblex, <em>the Faceless Lord</em>.</li><li>Archdevils: Asmodeus, <em>Lord of the Nine Hells</em>; Zariel, <em>Lord of Avernus</em>.</li></ul><p>For a demon, wild and violent epithets fit (the Ravager, the Unbound). For a devil, go for rank and order: a Duke or Marquis of somewhere specific, with a title that sounds like a contract.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Building a full demonic title",
        "content": "<p>The most memorable demons stack their titles. Try name, then rank and domain, then an epithet:</p><ul><li><em>Vharzul, Duke of Ashes, the Unforgiving</em></li><li><em>Ozrakh the Devourer, Prince of the Sunless Sea</em></li></ul><p>Two titles is plenty for most demons; save the long string for the one at the end of the campaign. And let mortals use the short version: they're afraid to say the rest.</p><p>For where they come from, try the <a href=\"demon-realm-name-generator.html\">Demon Realm Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is a demon epithet?",
        "answer": "A title added to a demon's name that describes its power, sin or domain, like \"the Devourer\" or \"Lord of the Flies\". Demons in folklore, religious texts and fantasy often have several."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where do demon ranks like Duke and Marquis come from?",
        "answer": "From grimoires like the Ars Goetia, which gives each of its 72 demons a noble rank: king, prince, duke, marquis, president, earl or knight. The Rank and domain option builds titles the same way."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between a demon and a devil?",
        "answer": "In D&D, demons are chaotic creatures of the Abyss and devils are orderly creatures of the Nine Hells. In older Christian writing the words are used much more loosely."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I give a demon more than one epithet?",
        "answer": "Yes, and many famous ones have several. A rank and domain plus one epithet (\"Duke of Ashes, the Unforgiving\") is a good balance."
      }
    ]
